WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.077 --> 00:00:01.977
- [Evan] Good morning
Believe Nation, it's Evan.

00:00:01.977 --> 00:00:03.363
My one word is believe

00:00:03.363 --> 00:00:04.476
And I believe in people

00:00:04.476 --> 00:00:06.256
more than they believe in themselves.

00:00:06.256 --> 00:00:09.160
And my sincere hope is
that if you see in yourself

00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:12.283
what I see in you, you'll be
able to change the planet.

00:00:12.283 --> 00:00:13.523
So to help you on your journey,

00:00:13.523 --> 00:00:16.589
today's message is schedule your time.

00:00:16.589 --> 00:00:18.256
Over to you Tim Ferriss.

00:00:18.256 --> 00:00:20.489
(rooster crows)

00:00:20.489 --> 00:00:23.266
♫ I wake up every morning

00:00:23.266 --> 00:00:25.856
♫ Espresso keep me going

00:00:25.856 --> 00:00:29.944
♫ I wake up every morning

00:00:29.944 --> 00:00:32.533
- Large uninterrupted blocks of time

00:00:32.533 --> 00:00:34.894
you have to schedule if you are a creator.

00:00:34.894 --> 00:00:35.958
Blocks of time that are at least

00:00:35.958 --> 00:00:38.935
two to four hours or more in length.

00:00:38.935 --> 00:00:42.641
No frankenstein monster
of 20-minute breaks and

00:00:42.641 --> 00:00:45.148
10-minute breaks combined into 3-hours

00:00:45.148 --> 00:00:47.622
will have the value of an uninteruppted

00:00:47.622 --> 00:00:49.345
block of three hours.

00:00:49.345 --> 00:00:51.806
If you are trying to make
high-level decisions,

00:00:51.806 --> 00:00:56.419
focus on time-consuming
high priority projects,

00:00:56.419 --> 00:00:58.337
push something to the next milestone.

00:00:58.337 --> 00:01:00.883
We all have the same 24 hours in the day

00:01:00.883 --> 00:01:01.716
and if you're like

00:01:01.716 --> 00:01:03.052
I don't have time, I can't sleep,

00:01:03.052 --> 00:01:04.033
I need to cut back on sleep,

00:01:04.033 --> 00:01:05.610
I need to do this, I need to do that,

00:01:05.610 --> 00:01:08.733
If you don't have time,
you don't have priorities.

00:01:08.733 --> 00:01:09.566
That's it.

00:01:09.566 --> 00:01:11.244
- I never use I don't have time

00:01:11.244 --> 00:01:13.033
as a reason for not to do something.

00:01:13.033 --> 00:01:13.866
I'm allergic to it.

00:01:13.866 --> 00:01:16.912
As soon as you start
saying I don't have time

00:01:16.912 --> 00:01:18.297
to do something,

00:01:18.297 --> 00:01:21.321
then you're giving responsibility
away to something else.

00:01:21.321 --> 00:01:22.784
You don't have time, it's not your fault.

00:01:22.784 --> 00:01:23.622
You just don't have the time.

00:01:23.622 --> 00:01:24.849
It's somebody else's fault.

00:01:24.849 --> 00:01:26.982
It's this project's fault,
this person's fault.

00:01:26.982 --> 00:01:29.280
It's always somebody else's fault.

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:33.197
Until you take control
of your responsibilities

00:01:33.197 --> 00:01:35.983
and your priorities and
managing your own time

00:01:35.983 --> 00:01:37.160
you're never going to go off and do

00:01:37.160 --> 00:01:39.407
the big things that you want to do.

00:01:39.407 --> 00:01:41.425
Especially the people who complain that

00:01:41.425 --> 00:01:43.370
you have this big goal and this big dream

00:01:43.370 --> 00:01:45.064
you want to build this huge company

00:01:45.064 --> 00:01:46.724
and then you're not doing it.

00:01:46.724 --> 00:01:48.539
You're not spending the time to do it.

00:01:48.539 --> 00:01:50.329
Because it's so great
to live up in our heads

00:01:50.329 --> 00:01:52.237
of all the things we want to do,

00:01:52.237 --> 00:01:56.267
of the passions, and the way
that our life could look.

00:01:56.267 --> 00:01:58.980
But you don't want to do the
actual work to get there.

00:01:58.980 --> 00:02:00.680
And now I get it. I get it.

00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:03.589
I'm a father. I'm a husband.

00:02:03.589 --> 00:02:05.959
I have three businesses
that I run at the same time.

00:02:05.959 --> 00:02:07.205
I'm a busy guy.

00:02:07.205 --> 00:02:11.632
But if I want to work on
something and I can't,

00:02:11.632 --> 00:02:13.407
then it's my problem.

00:02:13.407 --> 00:02:15.094
Right? It's nobody else's problem.

00:02:15.094 --> 00:02:17.490
It's not you guys. It's not Believe Nation

00:02:17.490 --> 00:02:20.468
expecting my video to come out.

00:02:20.468 --> 00:02:22.632
I choose to make YouTube videos.

00:02:22.632 --> 00:02:24.001
I choose to do three a day.

00:02:24.001 --> 00:02:26.196
I choose to spend my time on these things.

00:02:26.196 --> 00:02:29.218
And by very definition by
choosing to do something

00:02:29.218 --> 00:02:31.257
you're choosing not to do something else.

00:02:31.257 --> 00:02:33.139
I have control.

00:02:33.139 --> 00:02:34.994
You have control over your time as well.

00:02:34.994 --> 00:02:36.999
And the realities of paying the bills and

00:02:36.999 --> 00:02:39.689
family life and all that stuff,

00:02:39.689 --> 00:02:42.386
and it may force you to make
some difficult decisions.

00:02:42.386 --> 00:02:46.042
But as soon as you say I don't have time

00:02:46.042 --> 00:02:47.953
it means you're never going to make it.

00:02:47.953 --> 00:02:49.437
Because you're never going to have time.

00:02:49.437 --> 00:02:51.489
Because the people who constantly live in

00:02:51.489 --> 00:02:54.600
when this clears up, when
this thing goes away,

00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:55.976
then I'll have time.

00:02:55.976 --> 00:02:58.427
And then when that clears
away, something else comes up.

00:02:58.427 --> 00:03:00.209
When that's done, then I'll have time.

00:03:00.209 --> 00:03:02.037
And then you never have time.

00:03:02.037 --> 00:03:03.806
It's never the perfect time.

00:03:03.806 --> 00:03:05.978
You just have to go and start doing it.

00:03:05.978 --> 00:03:07.679
Nothing actually starts happening until

00:03:07.679 --> 00:03:09.980
you put it into your calendar
and you start taking action.

00:03:09.980 --> 00:03:11.296
Otherwise you're just living in your head

00:03:11.296 --> 00:03:12.944
and those dreams never come out.

00:03:12.944 --> 00:03:14.866
And so I would encourage you to

00:03:14.866 --> 00:03:17.396
any time you say I don't have time,

00:03:17.396 --> 00:03:19.378
to catch yourself and say

00:03:19.378 --> 00:03:21.710
I choose not to make it a priority.

00:03:21.710 --> 00:03:23.932
This is not a priority in my life.

00:03:23.932 --> 00:03:26.562
When you start embracing
that and actually feeling it

00:03:26.562 --> 00:03:28.355
then you'll start making
some important decisions

00:03:28.355 --> 00:03:29.889
on how you schedule your time.

00:03:29.889 --> 00:03:32.195
So to think what actually
is a priority to you,

00:03:32.195 --> 00:03:34.962
start making some tweaks
and you'll start to see

00:03:34.962 --> 00:03:36.996
some big changes happening in your life

00:03:36.996 --> 00:03:38.007
and in your business.

00:03:38.007 --> 00:03:40.436
Now to Tim's point about
creating a schedule,

00:03:40.436 --> 00:03:42.671
one of the things that
I find really helpful

00:03:42.671 --> 00:03:45.164
is chunking my time. We've
talked about this before.

00:03:45.164 --> 00:03:49.331
But I like having dedicated
days to do dedicated things.

00:03:50.516 --> 00:03:52.970
You know, I film once a
month for my YouTube channel

00:03:52.970 --> 00:03:54.030
for the most part.

00:03:54.030 --> 00:03:57.345
And we come in on a
Wednesday and film everything

00:03:57.345 --> 00:03:58.769
in one sitting.

00:03:58.769 --> 00:04:00.617
And I'm in video mode.

00:04:00.617 --> 00:04:02.926
I find it really hard to switch tasks.

00:04:02.926 --> 00:04:03.865
I think most people do.

00:04:03.865 --> 00:04:05.369
But I just find it really hard.

00:04:05.369 --> 00:04:08.999
I can't go from meeting with
somebody for half an hour

00:04:08.999 --> 00:04:11.247
to then writing a book for half an hour

00:04:11.247 --> 00:04:13.487
to then getting on a phone
call for half an hour

00:04:13.487 --> 00:04:15.921
to then recording a
video for half an hour.

00:04:15.921 --> 00:04:17.977
The switching costs are too high.

00:04:17.977 --> 00:04:19.373
If I'm going to write my book or

00:04:19.373 --> 00:04:22.185
do something else that's really creative,

00:04:22.185 --> 00:04:25.103
I need half an hour to forty
minutes just to get into it.

00:04:25.103 --> 00:04:28.080
Before I'm actually really
creating something good.

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:29.766
It's why when I make my videos here

00:04:29.766 --> 00:04:31.594
I start with the easy stuff first and then

00:04:31.594 --> 00:04:33.206
I lead to the Entspressos.

00:04:33.206 --> 00:04:35.847
Because it allows me to
warm up, to get going.

00:04:35.847 --> 00:04:37.764
If I have to come in and
boom, just drop an Entspresso

00:04:37.764 --> 00:04:40.253
it'd be a lot more challenging for me.

00:04:40.253 --> 00:04:42.596
And so anything you do
multiple times per week,

00:04:42.596 --> 00:04:43.506
try to do months for week,

00:04:43.506 --> 00:04:44.471
multiple times per month,

00:04:44.471 --> 00:04:45.889
once per month, and so on.

00:04:45.889 --> 00:04:47.414
Multiple times per day, once per day.

00:04:47.414 --> 00:04:50.012
Right? Like, email. Social media.

00:04:50.012 --> 00:04:51.584
If you're doing that all day long,

00:04:51.584 --> 00:04:53.706
you do that once per day.

00:04:53.706 --> 00:04:56.271
It means that you miss
out on some small things.

00:04:56.271 --> 00:04:58.492
It means that you miss out on
getting some opportunities.

00:04:58.492 --> 00:05:00.287
Maybe some reporter wants to interview you

00:05:00.287 --> 00:05:02.292
and has a deadline by 3pm today

00:05:02.292 --> 00:05:04.317
and you don't open that
email until 5 o'clock,

00:05:04.317 --> 00:05:05.909
you've missed it.

00:05:05.909 --> 00:05:08.324
But you have to be willing
to let go of the little

00:05:08.324 --> 00:05:10.220
to be able to go for the big.

00:05:10.220 --> 00:05:11.929
And I used to pride myself as the guy

00:05:11.929 --> 00:05:13.960
who responded to email super fast.

00:05:13.960 --> 00:05:15.438
You sent me an email,
like two minutes later

00:05:15.438 --> 00:05:16.458
boom, response.

00:05:16.458 --> 00:05:17.793
I would drop whatever I was doing

00:05:17.793 --> 00:05:20.041
to respond to your email.

00:05:20.041 --> 00:05:22.996
And what it meant was I was
doing lots of little tasks

00:05:22.996 --> 00:05:25.027
really quickly, but I never got to

00:05:25.027 --> 00:05:28.005
focus on the big task because
I kept getting distracted.

00:05:28.005 --> 00:05:30.914
And so by chunking your time,

00:05:30.914 --> 00:05:33.485
and by blocking off
access in your calendar,

00:05:33.485 --> 00:05:35.927
it means you'll have to
say no to some things,

00:05:35.927 --> 00:05:38.557
some little projects, some
things will slip through.

00:05:38.557 --> 00:05:39.623
But you're spending your time on

00:05:39.623 --> 00:05:41.229
the big projects that matter.

00:05:41.229 --> 00:05:44.200
And so it's been probably
the single most important

00:05:44.200 --> 00:05:48.132
time-management skill or
practice that I've applied,

00:05:48.132 --> 00:05:50.839
is chunking your time,
having one main thing

00:05:50.839 --> 00:05:53.018
that I do every single day,

00:05:53.018 --> 00:05:57.411
so you know Wednesday is
recording YouTube videos

00:05:57.411 --> 00:05:59.700
and when I'm not doing
that it's my Lily day

00:05:59.700 --> 00:06:01.670
where we're looking at her channel.

00:06:01.670 --> 00:06:03.530
Thursdays I have public facing day

00:06:03.530 --> 00:06:06.637
and we're doing interviews and podcasts

00:06:06.637 --> 00:06:08.797
and radio shows and YouTube channels

00:06:08.797 --> 00:06:10.794
and just extrovert mode.

00:06:10.794 --> 00:06:11.907
Monday is my TDS day.

00:06:11.907 --> 00:06:13.346
If I'm here, I'm working with Alex,

00:06:13.346 --> 00:06:14.493
we're working through all the problems

00:06:14.493 --> 00:06:15.964
that we have to do with TDS.

00:06:15.964 --> 00:06:17.483
Looking at opportunities
where we're going to grow.

00:06:17.483 --> 00:06:19.593
I'm just in TDS mindset.

00:06:19.593 --> 00:06:22.058
Right and so every day
I'm doing something else.

00:06:22.058 --> 00:06:24.136
But it also means that I
will delay some things.

00:06:24.136 --> 00:06:26.816
So if it's something TDS-related

00:06:26.816 --> 00:06:28.510
and Alex sends it to me on Tuesday

00:06:28.510 --> 00:06:30.939
I'm probably not going
to get it to next Monday.

00:06:30.939 --> 00:06:32.463
And so part of that is training the team

00:06:32.463 --> 00:06:35.602
to say hey, we've got
to get it done on time

00:06:35.602 --> 00:06:38.360
so I can handle it at the right moment.

00:06:38.360 --> 00:06:40.167
But it also means that
sometimes we may miss

00:06:40.167 --> 00:06:41.816
windows of opportunities

00:06:41.816 --> 00:06:43.401
because I haven't seen it and

00:06:43.401 --> 00:06:46.054
it's not going to come
up again until Monday.

00:06:46.054 --> 00:06:48.652
And being okay with that,
being okay with dropping

00:06:48.652 --> 00:06:52.279
some small balls so you can
keep the big ones in the air.

00:06:52.279 --> 00:06:55.346
So the question today is I'm curious,

00:06:55.346 --> 00:06:57.194
how are you scheduling your time?

00:06:57.194 --> 00:07:00.859
Do you prefer to have full
days or full afternoons

00:07:00.859 --> 00:07:02.672
dedicated to one thing at a time?

00:07:02.672 --> 00:07:04.886
Or do you like the variety and the mixup

00:07:04.886 --> 00:07:06.425
and spending half an hour on one thing

00:07:06.425 --> 00:07:07.800
and then moving to something else?

00:07:07.800 --> 00:07:08.975
Really curious to find about your habits.

00:07:08.975 --> 00:07:10.602
Leave it down in the comments below.

00:07:10.602 --> 00:07:11.944
Super curious to see what you have to say.

00:07:11.944 --> 00:07:13.375
And if you leave a comment
in the first few hours

00:07:13.375 --> 00:07:14.416
of this video going live,

00:07:14.416 --> 00:07:17.385
you have a chance to win
one of our two daily prizes.

00:07:17.385 --> 00:07:19.456
I also want to give a quick
shoutout to Dilip Gehlot,

00:07:19.456 --> 00:07:21.306
thank you so much for
picking up a copy of my book

00:07:21.306 --> 00:07:23.074
Your One Word, and taking that picture

00:07:23.074 --> 00:07:24.179
and sharing it with me.

00:07:24.179 --> 00:07:25.936
I love the background of Moana.

00:07:25.936 --> 00:07:27.441
I actually haven't seen that movie yet.

00:07:27.441 --> 00:07:29.138
Both Nina and Hayden have seen it

00:07:29.138 --> 00:07:30.474
and they don't want to watch it again

00:07:30.474 --> 00:07:33.044
and so I either have to watch it by myself

00:07:33.044 --> 00:07:35.636
or force somebody to watch it with me.

00:07:35.636 --> 00:07:37.232
I don't like watching cartoons by myself

00:07:37.232 --> 00:07:38.383
so that's the only issue.

00:07:38.383 --> 00:07:39.216
We'll see how it goes.

00:07:39.216 --> 00:07:41.177
But thank you so much for
picking up a copy of the book

00:07:41.177 --> 00:07:42.358
and for sharing it!

00:07:42.358 --> 00:07:44.086
I really appreciate the support.

00:07:44.086 --> 00:07:45.378
Thank you guys again for watching.

00:07:45.378 --> 00:07:46.596
I believe in you.

00:07:46.596 --> 00:07:48.573
I hope you continue to believe in yourself

00:07:48.573 --> 00:07:50.375
and whatever your one word is.

00:07:50.375 --> 00:07:52.780
Much love, and I'll see
you again tomorrow morning

00:07:52.780 --> 00:07:55.211
for another shot of Entspresso.

00:07:55.211 --> 00:07:57.952
♫ I wake up every morning

00:07:57.952 --> 00:08:01.369
♫ Espresso keep me going

00:08:04.650 --> 00:08:07.271
- [Neil] You know I'm very
methodical about time-management

00:08:07.271 --> 00:08:08.522
because it's so hard to do.

00:08:08.522 --> 00:08:10.157
So the first thing, I'll give you a couple

00:08:10.157 --> 00:08:11.475
important life-changing tips to

00:08:11.475 --> 00:08:12.324
anyone trying to do something creative

00:08:12.324 --> 00:08:13.773
on their computer.

00:08:13.773 --> 00:08:18.419
So number one thing is
no email in the morning.

00:08:18.419 --> 00:08:19.782
Like number one thing is there's a program

00:08:19.782 --> 00:08:22.672
called Freedom that I downloaded

00:08:22.672 --> 00:08:23.511
that it says,

00:08:23.511 --> 00:08:25.313
I love the word Freedom because

00:08:25.313 --> 00:08:26.146
that's really what it is.

00:08:26.146 --> 00:08:27.991
It says how many minutes
of freedom do you want?

00:08:27.991 --> 00:08:29.161
You say whatever it is, 90 minutes

00:08:29.161 --> 00:08:31.447
and then you cannot access
the internet for 90 minutes.

00:08:31.447 --> 00:08:32.770
There's no unlock code,

00:08:32.770 --> 00:08:34.922
people are gasping, you're
all going to use it.

00:08:34.922 --> 00:08:35.755
There's no unlock codes.

00:08:35.755 --> 00:08:38.930
The first thing I do is phone,

00:08:38.930 --> 00:08:40.047
I give to someone else.

00:08:40.047 --> 00:08:40.880
Or put it in a drawer somewhere.

00:08:40.880 --> 00:08:42.818
It's best if I give it to someone else.

00:08:42.818 --> 00:08:45.167
Internet goes off and I commit to

00:08:45.167 --> 00:08:46.334
that certain amount of time.

00:08:46.334 --> 00:08:48.623
So my thing is compartmentalizing.

00:08:48.623 --> 00:08:49.771
That if you're going to check your email,

00:08:49.771 --> 00:08:52.112
check it from four to five.

00:08:52.112 --> 00:08:53.826
Someone wrote a book which I've never read

00:08:53.826 --> 00:08:55.952
but it's called Never Check
Your Email in the Morning.

00:08:55.952 --> 00:08:57.248
I don't know if you read or not

00:08:57.248 --> 00:09:00.771
but just know the title
and it's really great.

00:09:00.771 --> 00:09:03.005
The thing is, with time is to become

00:09:03.005 --> 00:09:05.030
proactive about time instead of reactive

00:09:05.030 --> 00:09:07.296
because I know so many
people who can't do anything

00:09:07.296 --> 00:09:09.717
because you get a message on Facebook

00:09:09.717 --> 00:09:11.945
and then you're getting an email

00:09:11.945 --> 00:09:13.137
and then you're just reacting to that

00:09:13.137 --> 00:09:14.224
because it feels like you're
getting something done

00:09:14.224 --> 00:09:16.054
and at the end of the day
you've got nothing done.

00:09:16.054 --> 00:09:17.667
What my thing is that I'm really,

00:09:17.667 --> 00:09:19.333
and everyone knows that
I'm proactive about that

00:09:19.333 --> 00:09:21.345
to the degree that, by the way

00:09:21.345 --> 00:09:23.571
I got Facebook and Twitter
blocked on my computer.

00:09:23.571 --> 00:09:26.999
What I did is I downloaded
Intego ContentBarrier.

00:09:26.999 --> 00:09:29.724
Is it ContentBarrier? Whatever it is.

00:09:29.724 --> 00:09:32.251
- RescueTime can be used.

00:09:32.251 --> 00:09:34.126
NetNanny can be used to
block specific sites.

00:09:34.126 --> 00:09:35.109
- Yeah, and I had someone else

00:09:35.109 --> 00:09:36.969
put in their password for those sites

00:09:36.969 --> 00:09:37.853
so I didn't even know the password

00:09:37.853 --> 00:09:41.336
so I couldn't get to Twitter
or Facebook if I tried.

00:09:41.336 --> 00:09:43.164
So finding those things that are those,

00:09:43.164 --> 00:09:44.696
and put in walls between you and them

00:09:44.696 --> 00:09:45.999
or limiting them in certain ways.

00:09:45.999 --> 00:09:49.713
There's one other thing
I was going to say was,

00:09:49.713 --> 00:09:51.033
friends can be a pain in the ass

00:09:51.033 --> 00:09:52.268
if you're trying to be creative.

00:09:52.268 --> 00:09:53.439
And if you have more than seven friends

00:09:53.439 --> 00:09:54.427
and everyone wants to
see you one day a week

00:09:54.427 --> 00:09:55.723
what do you do?

00:09:55.723 --> 00:09:57.115
So another thing I want
to say is that I do

00:09:57.115 --> 00:09:59.335
a Wednesday night dinner party.

00:09:59.335 --> 00:10:00.286
So if my friends want to see me,

00:10:00.286 --> 00:10:01.233
I'm like great, Wednesday.

00:10:01.233 --> 00:10:02.809
Somebody wants a meeting
who I'm not sure about,

00:10:02.809 --> 00:10:03.642
Wednesday night.

00:10:03.642 --> 00:10:06.691
So Wednesday night, we
all go to a restaurant

00:10:06.691 --> 00:10:09.418
and basically whoever I
want to see or catch up on

00:10:09.418 --> 00:10:11.058
I'll see them there and
then maybe afterward

00:10:11.058 --> 00:10:12.280
I'll break off with my closer friends

00:10:12.280 --> 00:10:14.134
but that way I can see everyone.

00:10:14.134 --> 00:10:15.118
People who I want to meet with but I don't

00:10:15.118 --> 00:10:16.329
really want to take the
take time for a lunch

00:10:16.329 --> 00:10:18.265
or meeting one-on-one and
just bring them there.

00:10:18.265 --> 00:10:19.635
If I like them, I talk to them a bunch.

00:10:19.635 --> 00:10:20.961
If I don't, I put them far away.

00:10:20.961 --> 00:10:23.255
- [Tiesto] I mean, it depends.
If I have some time off

00:10:23.255 --> 00:10:24.679
I always take time for music.

00:10:24.679 --> 00:10:26.663
So for me, it's different I think.

00:10:26.663 --> 00:10:28.458
But when I'm touring every day,

00:10:28.458 --> 00:10:29.917
I actually have more routine than when

00:10:29.917 --> 00:10:32.539
I'm not touring every day.

00:10:32.539 --> 00:10:34.432
Because you have to have your schedules.

00:10:34.432 --> 00:10:36.893
So you really have to plan your day well.

00:10:36.893 --> 00:10:38.885
But if I have six weeks off,

00:10:38.885 --> 00:10:41.719
I'm pretty much all over
the place, you know?

00:10:41.719 --> 00:10:43.686
Yeah, you don't really have a schedule,

00:10:43.686 --> 00:10:45.096
you just do whatever.

00:10:45.096 --> 00:10:46.241
- [Offscreen] Yeah, and you waste time.

00:10:46.241 --> 00:10:48.944
- Yeah! But if you have a
really military schedule

00:10:48.944 --> 00:10:51.351
then you have to really
plan everything well.

00:10:51.351 --> 00:10:53.359
So that works better for me actually.

